00:10:38.437 --> 00:10:40.172
dampens the response
in the amygdala,

00:10:40.272 --> 00:10:42.875
which is the threat-detecting
sensor of the brain.

00:10:42.975 --> 00:10:45.744
Meaning you're able to confront
and process trauma

00:10:45.844 --> 00:10:47.279
that you can't otherwise face.

00:10:47.379 --> 00:10:49.714
Here is one researcher
explaining how it works

00:10:49.815 --> 00:10:52.150
with a story about taking
MDMA recreationally

00:10:52.250 --> 00:10:53.385
in his younger days.

00:10:53.618 --> 00:10:56.522
BEN SESSA:<i> I can remember
 at one time, after a rave,</i>

00:10:56.622 --> 00:10:58.657
<i> all of us lying around
 all loved up,</i>

00:10:58.891 --> 00:11:00.125
<i> and somebody said,</i>

00:11:00.225 --> 00:11:02.628
<i> "Oh, wow, this is amazing,
 it's so beautiful,</i>

00:11:02.728 --> 00:11:03.895
<i> everything's so perfect."</i>

00:11:03.995 --> 00:11:05.331
<i> And someone said,
 "Let's think of</i>

00:11:05.431 --> 00:11:07.566
<i> the worst possible thing
 we can imagine."</i>

00:11:07.666 --> 00:11:10.869
<i> And someone said,
"Let's imagine our moms dying."</i>

00:11:11.236 --> 00:11:13.972
<i> And then someone said,
 "It's not that bad."</i>

00:11:14.206 --> 00:11:16.775 line:0%
And I didn't know it then,

00:11:16.875 --> 00:11:20.179 line:0%
but that really was
what MDMA does.

00:11:20.279 --> 00:11:24.450 line:0%
It allows you to think about
and go to

00:11:24.550 --> 00:11:28.654 line:0%
those dark, difficult,
forbidden, avoidant memories

00:11:28.754 --> 00:11:30.222
that you normally
wouldn't be able to touch.

00:11:30.822 --> 00:11:32.224
That is fascinating.

00:11:32.324 --> 00:11:34.626
And I'm so glad that
that worked out for him,

00:11:34.726 --> 00:11:36.962
but "Let's imagine
our moms dying,"

00:11:37.062 --> 00:11:39.198
doesn't sound like
an amazing time.

00:11:39.498 --> 00:11:42.301
It sounds like a rave
DJ'd by Werner Herzog.

00:11:42.401 --> 00:11:43.935
(IMITATING WERNER HERZOG)
"Life is fleeting.

00:11:44.035 --> 00:11:47.339
Let's imagine our moms dying
as a solemn reminder

00:11:47.439 --> 00:11:48.974
that all we have is tonight.

00:11:49.074 --> 00:11:52.711
Now, wave your hands in the air
as if our slow passage

00:11:52.811 --> 00:11:55.714
towards oblivion
makes you just not care."

00:11:55.948 --> 00:11:58.250
(AIR HORN BLARES)

00:11:58.350 --> 00:12:01.954
Now, to be clear,
psychedelic therapy

00:12:02.054 --> 00:12:04.323
involves much more
than just taking a pill.

00:12:04.423 --> 00:12:06.391
These drugs alone
don't automatically

00:12:06.491 --> 00:12:07.426
produce results.

00:12:07.526 --> 00:12:09.128
Usually, treatment entails
multiple sessions

00:12:09.228 --> 00:12:10.195
lasting most of the day.

00:12:10.295 --> 00:12:12.531
In which you may take MDMA
or psilocybin

00:12:12.631 --> 00:12:14.667
under the supervision
of medical professionals,

00:12:14.767 --> 00:12:17.336
and then follow up
with processing and after care.

00:12:17.436 --> 00:12:21.039
They are not a replacement
for conventional talk therapy.

00:12:21.139 --> 00:12:22.174
They just seem to make you

00:12:22.274 --> 00:12:24.243
significantly more receptive
to it.

00:12:24.610 --> 00:12:26.178
Just watch
this former firefighter

00:12:26.278 --> 00:12:28.214
narrate video of himself
undergoing

00:12:28.314 --> 00:12:29.714
MDMA assisted therapy.

00:12:30.148 --> 00:12:32.551
This is your first
medicated treatment session?

00:12:32.651 --> 00:12:35.287
Correct. This is the beginning
of some interesting revelations

00:12:35.387 --> 00:12:36.621
on this video.

00:12:36.988 --> 00:12:39.658
And are you feeling
completely conscious
and you're in control and...?

00:12:39.758 --> 00:12:42.827 line:0%
Yeah. And I felt like
I was able to relax

00:12:42.927 --> 00:12:44.763 line:0%
even when I was talking
through probably

00:12:44.863 --> 00:12:46.865
my most traumatic memories.

00:12:46.965 --> 00:12:49.301
I don't think I had seen
a dead person until then.

00:12:50.035 --> 00:12:51.570
The first one
was gruesome too.

00:12:51.670 --> 00:12:54.439
It was like being able
to take a big breath, you know,

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:57.443
after being stuck under water
for a long time.

00:12:57.543 --> 00:12:58.577
Yeah.

00:12:58.677 --> 00:13:00.979
And you can see the utility
of having two people

00:13:01.079 --> 00:13:02.180
either side of that man

00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:03.516
talking him through
the experience.

00:13:03.616 --> 00:13:04.983
And if it sounds like
I'm treating

00:13:05.083 --> 00:13:06.951
having two people to talk to
about your feelings

00:13:07.052 --> 00:13:09.054
like a radical idea,
you do have to remember

00:13:09.154 --> 00:13:11.690
that I was born tragically
and medically British.

00:13:11.790 --> 00:13:12.857
(AUDIENCE LAUGHS)

00:13:12.958 --> 00:13:14.793
What is interesting is, though,

00:13:14.893 --> 00:13:17.295
it is not just
that your fear is muted.

00:13:17.396 --> 00:13:19.064
Your brain's ability
to make other connections

00:13:19.164 --> 00:13:21.433
and forge new neural pathways
is also heightened.

00:13:21.533 --> 00:13:24.169
Meaning that these sessions
can fundamentally alter

00:13:24.269 --> 00:13:25.637
the way you process things.

00:13:25.737 --> 00:13:26.838
That is thought to be
one reason

00:13:26.939 --> 00:13:28.540
why psychedelics
are so promising

00:13:28.640 --> 00:13:31.176
for treating conditions
like addiction and depression.

00:13:31.276 --> 00:13:33.612
And why they are proving
especially useful

00:13:33.712 --> 00:13:34.813
for dealing with trauma.

00:13:34.913 --> 00:13:36.715
That is what makes
this form of therapy

00:13:36.815 --> 00:13:38.584
particular appealing
to veterans.

00:13:38.784 --> 00:13:41.253
As PTSD is
an urgent issue for them.

00:13:45.591 --> 00:13:48.561
A rate substantially higher
from the general population.

00:13:48.661 --> 00:13:51.363
And it's not like the VA's
current treatment methods

00:13:51.463 --> 00:13:52.897
are particularly effective.

00:13:53.098 --> 00:13:54.699
Remember the guy from earlier
who compared

00:13:54.799 --> 00:13:57.269
psychedelic treatment
to a "bathtub full of puppies"?

00:13:57.603 --> 00:13:59.571
Listen to him describe
the mental health care

00:13:59.671 --> 00:14:01.940
from the VA
that he initially received.

00:14:02.440 --> 00:14:04.944
I drove to Womack Army
Medical Center at Fort Bragg.

00:14:06.111 --> 00:14:07.746
<i> And I said
 I was gonna kill myself.</i>

00:14:09.214 --> 00:14:10.448 line:0%
They gave me six Xanax

00:14:10.549 --> 00:14:12.484 line:0%
and told me not to take them all
at the same time

00:14:12.584 --> 00:14:14.152 line:0%
because it might kill me.

00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:16.355
They asked if
I had guns at home

00:14:16.455 --> 00:14:17.555
and I said, "Yes, a lot."

00:14:17.655 --> 00:14:20.392
And they asked if I had ammo,
I said, "Yeah, plenty."

00:14:20.892 --> 00:14:22.528
They said, "Okay,
when you get home,

00:14:22.628 --> 00:14:24.029
give all your guns
to a neighbor."

00:14:24.129 --> 00:14:25.764
This is like four o'clock
in the morning.

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:30.336
And they said,
"Come back after the holidays."

00:14:30.802 --> 00:14:31.904
Okay.

00:14:32.004 --> 00:14:34.573
I knew the VA's mental health
services were subpar,

00:14:34.673 --> 00:14:35.974
but I didn't know
they stooped to,

00:14:36.074 --> 00:14:37.543
"Here's a bunch of ways
to kill yourself,

00:14:37.643 --> 00:14:39.611
but honor system, don't,
and anyway,

00:14:39.712 --> 00:14:41.980
see you after the saddest time
of the year."

00:14:42.847 --> 00:14:45.618
Thankfully, that guy
saw such a profound change

00:14:45.718 --> 00:14:46.852
from MDMA therapy

00:14:46.952 --> 00:14:48.553
that he is now
helping to lead a movement

00:14:48.653 --> 00:14:51.857
to allow psychedelics to be used
to treat conditions like PTSD,

00:14:51.957 --> 00:14:55.160
eliciting help from
Democratic lawmakers like AOC,

00:14:55.260 --> 00:14:56.795
and even Republican lawmakers

00:14:56.895 --> 00:14:59.198
like Rick Perry
and Dan Crenshaw.

00:14:59.298 --> 00:15:01.800
And that kind of
bipartisan consensus

00:15:01.900 --> 00:15:03.335
has brought us to
the tipping point

00:15:03.435 --> 00:15:05.737
that we're at right now
because so far, in the U.S.,

00:15:05.837 --> 00:15:08.506
these drugs have only
legally been available to people

00:15:08.606 --> 00:15:09.942
in clinical trials.

00:15:10.042 --> 00:15:11.977
But over the next year,
it is widely anticipated

00:15:12.077 --> 00:15:14.613
that the FDA will approve
MDMA for clinical use

00:15:14.713 --> 00:15:17.616
with psilocybin expected
to follow a few years later.

00:15:17.716 --> 00:15:19.618
And maybe even LSD one day.

00:15:19.718 --> 00:15:22.621
Meaning that we may be about
to finally realize

00:15:22.721 --> 00:15:25.057
some of the huge potential
of psychedelics,

00:15:25.157 --> 00:15:26.258
which is great,

00:15:26.358 --> 00:15:29.962
but it is worth being
very careful at this point

00:15:30.295 --> 00:15:32.531
to make sure that
we don't repeat past mistakes

00:15:32.631 --> 00:15:34.432
or indeed, make new ones.

00:15:34.733 --> 00:15:36.035
And those who work
in this field

00:15:36.135 --> 00:15:38.069
point to a few big areas
for caution.

00:15:38.169 --> 00:15:40.104
The first concerns
how psychedelics

00:15:40.205 --> 00:15:41.673
are administered,
and by whom.

00:15:41.773 --> 00:15:43.575
Because while people
have used these drugs

00:15:43.675 --> 00:15:45.744
recreationally forever
and will continue to

00:15:45.844 --> 00:15:47.779
at parties, at raves,
in nature,

00:15:47.879 --> 00:15:50.282
or to really liven up
a baptism,

00:15:51.583 --> 00:15:54.687
psychedelic assisted therapy
is not for everyone.

00:15:54.787 --> 00:15:56.021
For example,
anyone with a history

00:15:56.121 --> 00:15:57.623
of schizophrenia
or psychosis

00:15:57.723 --> 00:15:59.491
may want to steer clear.

00:15:59.591 --> 00:16:01.593
And even if you are
a good match,

00:16:01.693 --> 00:16:03.529
because these drugs
can put you in

00:16:03.629 --> 00:16:06.965
an incredibly vulnerable state,
experts emphasize the importance

00:16:07.065 --> 00:16:08.600
of trying them
in a controlled setting,

00:16:08.700 --> 00:16:11.603
which ideally would involve
two people present at all times,

00:16:11.703 --> 00:16:13.338
and even videotaping
the session,

00:16:13.438 --> 00:16:15.306
like that one
that you just saw earlier.

00:16:15.407 --> 00:16:17.509
They advise this
not just for your own safety,

00:16:17.609 --> 00:16:20.479
but also because for all
their potential benefits,

00:16:20.579 --> 00:16:23.748
these drugs aren't always
a bathtub full of puppies.

00:16:24.215 --> 00:16:27.118 line:0%
We tell people that
their experiences

00:16:27.218 --> 00:16:32.057 line:0%
may vary from very positive
to transcendent and lovely

00:16:32.157 --> 00:16:34.326
to literally
hell realm experiences.

00:16:34.426 --> 00:16:35.293
Hell realm?

00:16:35.594 --> 00:16:39.197
As frightening an experience
as you have ever had

00:16:39.297 --> 00:16:40.199
in your life.

00:16:40.432 --> 00:16:43.535
Now, that does sound bad,
although, to be fair,

00:16:43.635 --> 00:16:46.170
you don't need to take drugs
to get to the hell realm.

00:16:46.271 --> 00:16:49.241
After all, that is why
airports have Sbarros.

00:16:49.508 --> 00:16:54.146
But it is important to note
that even those in studies

00:16:54.246 --> 00:16:56.948
who've reported hell realm
type experiences

00:16:57.048 --> 00:16:59.417
have often still felt
that they benefited

00:16:59.517 --> 00:17:00.652
from the treatment.

00:17:00.752 --> 00:17:02.420
But safety is not
the only worry here.

00:17:02.521 --> 00:17:04.590
There are also concerns
over accessibility.

00:17:04.690 --> 00:17:07.526
These treatments are likely
to be very expensive.

00:17:07.626 --> 00:17:09.427
And there are some players
angling to make them

00:17:09.527 --> 00:17:10.461
even more so.

00:17:10.562 --> 00:17:12.130
Just look at
Compass Pathways.

00:17:12.230 --> 00:17:15.133
A for-profit psychedelic company
backed by Peter Thiel.

00:17:15.233 --> 00:17:16.268
It...

00:17:19.871 --> 00:17:21.406
...which already seems
pretty aggressive

00:17:21.506 --> 00:17:23.508
for a drug that, remember,
has been around

00:17:23.608 --> 00:17:25.277
for thousands of years.

00:17:25.611 --> 00:17:28.447
And Compass' executives
seemed pretty defensive

00:17:28.547 --> 00:17:29.914
when they were pressed
about this.

00:17:30.448 --> 00:17:33.184
Do you really believe
that your form of psilocybin

00:17:33.284 --> 00:17:34.753
is novel and non-obvious?

00:17:34.953 --> 00:17:37.622 line:0%
Well, it doesn't really matter
what we believe.

00:17:38.089 --> 00:17:41.092 line:0%
What does matter is what
the U.S. Patent Office believes,

00:17:41.192 --> 00:17:42.794
what the U.K. Patent Office
believes,

00:17:42.894 --> 00:17:44.762
what Germany,
what Hong Kong believes.

00:17:45.163 --> 00:17:48.166
And all of them believe
that actually it is

00:17:48.266 --> 00:17:49.868
because we have
the granted patents.

00:17:50.168 --> 00:17:51.169
Okay.

00:17:51.469 --> 00:17:53.672
But we shouldn't necessarily
trust the U.S. Patent Office

00:17:53.772 --> 00:17:55.474
as an arbiter
of good judgment.

00:17:55.574 --> 00:17:57.275
This is the same place
that issued patents for

00:17:57.375 --> 00:17:58.643
an umbrella for your beer,

00:17:58.744 --> 00:18:01.146
a tricycle with a lawnmower
attached to it,

00:18:01.246 --> 00:18:02.514
and a stick.

00:18:02.614 --> 00:18:05.450
That's it, just a stick
for animals to play with.

00:18:05.550 --> 00:18:07.319
Someone at the Patent Office
saw that and said,

00:18:07.419 --> 00:18:10.189
"We've never seen
a stick before. Approved!"

00:18:10.722 --> 00:18:12.824
So take what they believe
is important

00:18:12.924 --> 00:18:14.459
with a big pinch of salt.

00:18:14.560 --> 00:18:16.661
And look, it is not just
the drug formulation.

00:18:16.761 --> 00:18:18.663
Compass has also tried
to patent other aspects

00:18:18.763 --> 00:18:19.698
of what's often called,

00:18:19.798 --> 00:18:21.800
"the set and setting
of treatment."

00:18:21.900 --> 00:18:24.135
From the room comprising
soft furniture

00:18:24.235 --> 00:18:26.638
or being decorated using
muted colors,

00:18:26.738 --> 00:18:29.174
to the subjects lying
on a bed or on a couch,

00:18:29.274 --> 00:18:32.611
or a therapist responding
to things a patient is saying.

00:18:32.711 --> 00:18:35.847
Which is just the entire
fucking concept of therapy!

00:18:36.147 --> 00:18:37.849
It'd be like me patenting
the concept

00:18:37.949 --> 00:18:40.519
of wearing a suit
while sitting at a desk.

00:18:40.752 --> 00:18:42.721
I'm clearly not the only person
who does this.

00:18:42.821 --> 00:18:44.856
Now, if I were to patent
the concept of doing that

00:18:44.956 --> 00:18:46.892
while making you wonder
why the fuck your TV is still on

00:18:46.992 --> 00:18:48.827
after<i> The Last of Us</i> has ended,
then, yeah,

00:18:48.927 --> 00:18:51.096
that would be uniquely
my thing.

00:18:51.696 --> 00:18:54.332
Now, those set and setting
patent applications

00:18:54.432 --> 00:18:55.434
are still pending.

00:18:55.534 --> 00:18:57.735
But the very fact they applied
for them at all

00:18:57.836 --> 00:18:59.871
is a pretty clear sign
of just how aggressive

00:18:59.971 --> 00:19:02.507
companies in this space
intend to be going forward.

00:19:02.607 --> 00:19:04.476
And it is not like
these treatments

00:19:04.576 --> 00:19:06.178
are going to be cheap
to start with.

00:19:06.378 --> 00:19:08.980
Experts project a course
of psychedelic therapy

00:19:09.080 --> 00:19:12.284
is likely to cost upwards
of 15,000 dollars.

00:19:12.384 --> 00:19:13.251
And given that,

00:19:13.351 --> 00:19:14.886
as we've discussed before
on this show,

00:19:14.986 --> 00:19:16.354
there is currently
a massive shortage

00:19:16.455 --> 00:19:18.489
of mental health professionals
in this country,

00:19:18.589 --> 00:19:21.860
due in part to how fucked up
our health insurance system is,

00:19:22.060 --> 00:19:23.962
you can see how
this could quickly and easily

00:19:24.062 --> 00:19:25.530
become a big mess,

00:19:25.630 --> 00:19:29.167
or only accessible
to the wealthiest Americans.

00:19:29.434 --> 00:19:31.336
So there is a lot
to figure out here

00:19:31.436 --> 00:19:33.939
even before you get
into questions like:

00:19:34.039 --> 00:19:35.873
Who will regulate
psychedelic therapy?

00:19:35.973 --> 00:19:38.276
Because the FDA
is in charge of drugs,

00:19:38.376 --> 00:19:40.812
but it doesn't regulate
the practice of medicine.

00:19:40.912 --> 00:19:42.413
Now, state laws
could cover it,

00:19:42.513 --> 00:19:44.416
state regulatory agencies could,

00:19:44.516 --> 00:19:46.284
or professional
organizations might.

00:19:46.384 --> 00:19:48.086
But someone has to.

00:19:48.453 --> 00:19:51.489
Because if they don't,
people are likely to get hurt,

00:19:51.589 --> 00:19:53.958
which could then send us
all the way back

00:19:54.058 --> 00:19:57.195
to fearmongering, bans,
and research getting put on ice.

00:19:57.295 --> 00:19:59.964
Which is the last thing
that we should want to happen.

00:20:00.064 --> 00:20:02.100
Because there is massive
potential here.

00:20:02.300 --> 00:20:04.269
These treatments have shown
that they can sometimes

00:20:04.369 --> 00:20:06.138
have lasting effects.

00:20:06.238 --> 00:20:08.507
Remember the world's oldest
33-year-old

00:20:08.607 --> 00:20:09.807
that you saw earlier?

00:20:09.907 --> 00:20:12.678
Whose LSD treatments seem
to have curbed his alcoholism?

00:20:13.011 --> 00:20:15.546
CBS caught up with him
a quarter century later

00:20:15.646 --> 00:20:19.551
to see how things were going,
and the answer was, pretty well.

00:20:19.651 --> 00:20:23.021
ARTHUR KING:<i> LSD allowed me
 to see my own mind.</i>

00:20:23.121 --> 00:20:26.224
There was a problem that I had
to resolve, and I faced it,

00:20:26.324 --> 00:20:27.759
and it disappeared.

00:20:27.859 --> 00:20:29.060
<i> As far as I'm concerned,
 I wouldn't take</i>

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:30.729
<i> a million dollars
 for the peace of mind</i>

00:20:30.829 --> 00:20:33.298
<i> that I obtained
 from this therapy.</i>

00:20:33.398 --> 00:20:36.168
CBS REPORTER: It was that good,
that refreshing,

00:20:36.268 --> 00:20:38.303
-that rejuvenating.
-And that enduring.

00:20:38.770 --> 00:20:39.838
Exactly.

00:20:39.938 --> 00:20:42.340
Thirty years later,
he was still feeling benefits

00:20:42.440 --> 00:20:44.142
from the treatment,
which is striking.

00:20:44.242 --> 00:20:46.211
And you'd think
that we'd have acted

00:20:46.311 --> 00:20:47.745
on evidence like that,
but instead,

00:20:47.845 --> 00:20:49.448
it seems we've spent
the last half century

00:20:49.548 --> 00:20:51.349
terrifying people
with bogus science

00:20:51.449 --> 00:20:53.317
and giving meth to monkeys.

00:20:53.951 --> 00:20:56.821
But the good news is,
we are finally moving forward

00:20:56.921 --> 00:21:00.524
and hopefully we will do that
with real caution and real care.

00:21:00.758 --> 00:21:03.295
Because if we do, maybe,
just maybe,

00:21:03.395 --> 00:21:07.065
one day you will see ads
for psychedelic therapy on TV

